WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.819
Pete: Hey it's Pete.

00:00:01.819 --> 00:00:09.650
I'm in Shanghai China today and I'm at a very
cool space called Hackerspace where geeks

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:16.720
get together and play with various technology
including a lot of robotics related stuff.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.980
And today I have the pleasure of talking to
Ricky Yi, and Ricky's the founder of a company

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:27.669
called DFRobot and he's going to tell us little
bit about what they're up to in terms of making

00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:31.159
Arduino kits which is quite cool, actually.

00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:33.110
And, so we're really happy to be here.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:34.110
Thanks for coming.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.110
Ricky: Thank you

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:42.660
Pete: So, Ricky, first tell me a little about
DFRobot and the high-level of what you guys

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:43.660
do.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:50.269
Ricky: Right, actually DFRobot started from
2007 and we were probably the first company

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:52.690
to introduce Arduino in China.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:58.059
And then, we formed the official company in
2008 and started building Ardunio kits and

00:00:58.059 --> 00:00:59.539
robots.

00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:05.810
And the main target is for education and hobbyists
at the very beginning and then start growing

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.690
and target more industry-level focus.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.150
Pete: Great.

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:15.509
So just to set the stage, for all the geeks
out there who might not know what Arduino

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:20.329
is, tell us a little bit about what's cool
about Arduino and what the big deal is.

00:01:20.329 --> 00:01:26.850
Ricky: I think Arduino is the open-source
hardware, as everybody knows, and it's first

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.259
designed for interactive work.

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:38.930
So, the main purpose of Arduino is to bridge
the gap for software developers and the actual

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.369
physical world.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:47.290
So, it's the link between software and actual
world.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:52.649
So Arduino is an easy to use board which a
software programmer can just a simple click

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:57.820
a few buttons and burn the programs into the
board and then you have the capability to

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:05.740
control the actual sensors, to read data from
sensors, to control the motors.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.739
And with the motors you can do a lot of things,
it's just based on your imagination.

00:02:10.739 --> 00:02:11.940
Pete: Cool.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.530
You have an Arduino board here.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.740
I think this is one of your kits.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.650
Can you just show us briefly what the hardware
looks like?

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:27.930
Ricky: So this is the standard Arduino board,
and you can see it's very simple, and it's

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.980
elegant and beautiful.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.290
And here, this is our product, we call this
IO Expansion Shield.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:41.110
So what this does is to actually expands the
capability of Arduinos.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.080
So you can see it just simply stacks on.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.550
And with this board, you actually can see
there's a lot of pins out which expands the

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:50.530
pins for the Arduino.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:57.250
And also you have a wireless communication
socket which you can give the Arduino a wireless

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:03.670
communication capability, such as the XB modules
which is very popular in the industry.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:09.580
Also, this gives you IS 2,3,4 and 5 communication
ports as well.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:14.470
With this board it's very easy to expand the
capability of Arduino.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.720
And also it's designed for some kits like
this.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.500
This is a joystick module, right.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:26.720
So with this module you can do a lot of gaming,
you know motion control sort of things.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.860
But with Arduino it's not that easy - you
needed to hardwire a lot of cables to this

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:34.860
module.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:42.020
But with this IO Expansion Shield all you
need to do is plug on a pin and then write

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.450
some code.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.450
Job done.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:45.510
It's that kind of easy.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.510
Pete: That's great.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.439
So talk to me a little bit about the software
that you use to control these devices.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:54.750
Is that something very obscure or obtuse?

00:03:54.750 --> 00:04:00.660
Or is that - how does that work, when you
want to talk to an Arduino device with software.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.010
Ricky: Basically, we're using standard Arduino
IDE.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.580
It's derived from the Processing language.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:12.860
Basically it's a Java kind of style of programming
format.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:18.700
So, I think most programmers, software programmers
can pick that up very easily.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.830
Maybe in an hour or two hours.

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:24.330
What you need to do - it's just a standard
programming IDE.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.260
Write in and type codes.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:31.460
And what you do is just a standard compile
and upload, and job done.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.000
Pete: Ok, interesting.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.570
And now the Arduino platform is open-source.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.660
I guess I feel like I know what that means
when it comes down to open-source software.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.450
But what is open-source hardware?

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.050
What does that mean?

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:51.860
Ricky: Open-source hardware is to, they just
give you all the details about the hardware

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:59.480
such as the schematics to the design file,
probably the layout file, the firmware, also

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:02.390
the BOM which is the bill or material.

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:08.860
So with these four files actually can easy
to put this to a factory and they can manufacture

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.740
products based on this file.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:18.360
So for open-source hardware which means you
can very easy to copy and reproduce the product.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:26.680
Pete: So, one of these boards, an Arduino
board, costs around what $20 or so?

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:34.900
So if this board costs $20 and you don't want
to spend $20 for every piece of product you

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:39.820
deliver to a customer what you're saying is
you're able to send the specs, send this to

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:42.880
a factory where it can be mass-produced for
much cheaper.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.240
Ricky: Yeah.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:52.490
I think the most application so far is, say,
using Arduino to do rapid prototyping.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:58.871
And for mature products design a new board,
maybe based on Arduino, maybe not, for that

00:05:58.871 --> 00:06:03.270
purpose you can save a lot of cost because
all the files are open so it's very easy to

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:07.530
modify and reproduce the product.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.530
Pete: That's great.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:13.060
So it's basically - for those of us who do
startups and understand the whole concept

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:18.570
of rapid iteration, Arduino allows you to
do quicker cycles with hardware.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.610
Is that right?

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.000
Ricky: That's correct, yeah.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.490
Pete: So, when it comes down to real-world
products and applications is Arduino just

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.180
something for geeks who like robots?

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:33.470
Or is there some real-world applications that
you've seen in the market that are interesting?

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:42.280
Ricky: I mean, if you know Arduino you know
how Arduino is applied in various types of

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:53.120
works such as interactive art, education,
robotics, even home automation as well.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.850
But, that's the past.

00:06:55.850 --> 00:07:01.590
From last year we've had several customers
actually come to us looking for code developed

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.180
for an industry product.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:09.810
The recent finished product is called a stomach
probe product, which using an industry stomach

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:10.810
probe.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:11.810
Pete: A stomach probe?

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.650
Ricky: Yeah, stomach probe.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:20.000
So they actually have asked a company, so
a traditional company, using a PRC or a micro

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:30.830
controller to the development, and they figure
out the cost and the development time is not

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:33.950
a good thing.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:38.060
And such as for a standard one is like twelve
months.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:44.220
And for us, I mean we saved the time to one
month - we finished the project in one month.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:47.900
And then we saved the cost to about 1/5.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:48.900
Which is great.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:56.450
Pete: So there're arguably ways to put real
products in the market based on Arduino technology

00:07:56.450 --> 00:08:01.490
that already supports certain kinds of standards,
standards compliance, etc.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:06.200
Ricky: Yes, that's right because Arduino itself
has already passed all the tests.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.630
So the design is mature and reliable.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:15.430
And the second case is we recently working
with a company which is trying to design Segway

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:20.150
style transportation tools.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:27.580
And they designed for a ? actually, and they're
using Arduino as the main controller.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.800
And they sell 5,000 in a year for that product
based on Arduino.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:32.800
That's an amazing thing.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:40.190
Pete: Well I find it fascinating as an entrepreneur
and a geek myself that the Arduino platform

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:48.540
allows me to create hardware based solutions
and then send them off for manufacturing and,

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:56.320
potentially, even as a low capital style,
lean startup operation, produce a real-world

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.870
useful hardware based solution.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.100
That's fascinating.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:04.890
Ricky: Yeah, that's the biggest part of Arduino
- that's the spirit of Arduino.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:11.800
Using Arduino you can produce a lot of end
applications, not the half-product application.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:16.700
We think Arduino is, in the industry level,
it's just in the middle.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:22.400
It gives you the capability to do rapid prototyping,
to do research, but it's not used for the

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:23.400
end product.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:24.400
Pete: Right.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.240
So, do you think if I come up with a really
cool application, a hardware based application,

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.880
solution, that Foxconn will want my business?

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:31.880
Ricky: Yeah, definitely.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:37.630
If you have such an idea you can use Arduino
to do the prototyping and then they'll manufacture.

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:38.970
Pete: That's great - very cool.

